Parent: #12
Depends on: #36, #38, #39

## Root problem

The repository stores many relationships as unvalidated arrays of IDs and contains more than one product-graph implementation. Change impact is incomplete and partly hard-coded, so drawings, builds, analyses, validation, manufacturing outputs, and releases can remain incorrectly current after upstream changes.

## Outcome

Build one indexed typed product graph that connects every authoritative engineering object, validates relation integrity, answers traceability questions, and deterministically calculates affected and stale records after commands.

## Relation model

Define typed directed relations including:

- contains/part-of;
- derives/refines;
- satisfies/verifies;
- allocates-to;
- interfaces-with/exchanges-with;
- instantiated-from/represented-by;
- connected-to/placed-as/modeled-by;
- built-from/tested-by/generated-from;
- approved-by/reviewed-by;
- depends-on/blocks/conflicts-with;
- supersedes/affected-by-change;
- as-designed/as-planned/as-built/as-tested links.

Every relation must define allowed source/target types, ownership, cardinality, lifecycle behavior, deletion/replacement policy, and impact semantics.

## Required services

- graph write validation inside command transactions;
- forward/reverse adjacency indexes;
- path and neighborhood queries;
- trace matrices and coverage calculation;
- orphan/cycle/integrity diagnostics;
- dependency-specific invalidation rules;
- stale reason and source-command explanation;
- impact preview before command apply;
- cross-domain replacement and deletion analysis;
- graph snapshots bound to versions;
- permission-aware graph queries;
- graph visualization as a view, not a separate data model.

## UX requirements

- “why does this exist?” and “what depends on this?” views;
- trace and coverage matrices;
- impact preview in command/import/MCP/AI reviews;
- stale badges with exact upstream causes;
- cross-probe between linked representations;
- blocker explanations for release candidates;
- integrity-repair workflow.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] One graph service replaces all parallel graph implementations.
- [ ] Invalid relation types/cardinality cannot commit.
- [ ] A reference component or requirement change produces a deterministic affected set across electronics, PCB, mechanical, firmware, validation, drawings, artifacts, and release.
- [ ] Deletion/replacement cannot leave silent orphan references.
- [ ] Stale status includes reason, source command, affected version, and resolution path.
- [ ] Graph queries enforce project/workspace permissions.
- [ ] Version comparison can explain relation changes.

## Required tests

Relation invariant tests, path/coverage fixtures, deletion/replacement tests, cycles/orphans, permission isolation, performance on reference-scale graph, stale propagation golden tests, and browser cross-probe/impact-review tests.

## Non-goals

- treating graph visualization layout as the graph engine;
- using generic graph relations without domain semantics.

## Completion guard

Do not close after adding a graph library or drawing nodes. Closure requires canonical typed relations, indexes, command integration, impact/stale behavior, integrity repair, permissions, and full reference-workflow evidence.
